{"id":"seller-record-before-sale","title":"Orders, liens and non-bank loans against sellers before the City bought","question":"Did the buildings the City bought carry City orders, unpaid-bill liens or loans from non-bank lenders in the five years before the Board voted?","finding":"13 of the purchased properties carry such records in the five years before the vote: 601-617 Laguna Street (4 City lien (Public Utilities (water)); 3 City lien (Public Health); 3 order of abatement (Building Inspection)); 3055-3061 16th Street (1 order of abatement (Building Inspection); 6 loans from lenders that are not banks or government); 624 Laguna Street (3 City lien (Public Health); 1 mechanic's lien (a contractor)).","how":"Documents in the index recorded in the five years up to the Board vote: orders of abatement, notices of lien, mechanic's liens, notices of default and lis pendens (releases and revocations left out), and deeds of trust whose beneficiary is not a bank, credit union, insurer or government body. Lenders who are people are shown as 'a person' or 'a private trust'.","limits":"An order of abatement is a City demand to fix a code violation, usually later lifted; a lien for an unpaid water or health-permit bill is ordinary and often paid within weeks. Private and fund lenders are common for small commercial buildings. None of this says the price was wrong; it says what the City knew, or could have known, about the building's condition and the seller's finances. The appraisals and condition reports would settle it.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"building","t":"Building","type":"text"},{"k":"approved","t":"Board vote","type":"date"},{"k":"price","t":"Price","type":"usd"},{"k":"seller","t":"Seller (Board file)","type":"text"},{"k":"orders","t":"Orders of abatement","type":"int"},{"k":"liens","t":"Liens","type":"int"},{"k":"released","t":"Releases or revocations since","type":"int"},{"k":"records","t":"What was recorded","type":"text"},{"k":"nonBankLoans","t":"Non-ban